 project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/hdctools" ref="refs/changes/10/354610/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ifc934808c02aef45243e6a6ed099124cca4674d2" commit="f44615ce3cd9303e6212674562a005bfabd95c37" commit_message="devserver: Fix lint issues.  Fix many (not all) lint issues in this directory.  BUG=None TEST=run_unittests  Change-Id: Ifc934808c02aef45243e6a6ed099124cca4674d2 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/354735 Commit-Ready: Don Garrett &lt;dgarrett@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Don Garrett &lt;dgarrett@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Ningning Xia &lt;nxia@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="354735" owner_email="dgarrett@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform/dev-util" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/dev-util" ref="refs/changes/35/354735/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-4.4" change_id="I430753266c7dbe593cc5f5985b413472446709f1" commit="e1ec9b6ea5e9bef635b216576e2a8224a69df569" commit_message="CHROMIUM: alt-syscall: override setpriority syscall for Android  When running in the android container, Android needs to set the nice level favorably for some processes.  However this messes with the existing priority scheme in Chrome OS.  For instance we don't want to favor Android's audio threads over Chrome's.  We also shouldn't put Android's Display at a much higher level than the Chrome's.  To solve this, scale Android's favorable nice levels by a factor of two.  This makes puts Android's audio threads at the same value and display threads slightly less favorable than Chrome's  BUG=b/29188854 TEST=check thread prios while running audio from Android on Minnie.  Change-Id: I430753266c7dbe593cc5f5985b413472446709f1 Signed-off-by: Dylan Reid &lt;dgreid@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/353692 Reviewed-by: Zach Reizner &lt;zachr@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="353692" owner_email="dgreid@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/92/353692/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="I430753266c7dbe593cc5f5985b413472446709f1" commit="e885db4380f68e9d3b474c36a285c88076cc5961" commit_message="CHROMIUM: alt-syscall: override setpriority syscall for Android  When running in the android container, Android needs to set the nice level favorably for some processes.  However this messes with the existing priority scheme in Chrome OS.  For instance we don't want to favor Android's audio threads over Chrome's.  We also shouldn't put Android's Display at a much higher level than the Chrome's.  To solve this, scale Android's favorable nice levels by a factor of two.  This makes puts Android's audio threads at the same value and display threads slightly less favorable than Chrome's  BUG=b/29188854 TEST=check thread prios while running audio from Android on Minnie.  Change-Id: I430753266c7dbe593cc5f5985b413472446709f1 Signed-off-by: Dylan Reid &lt;dgreid@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/353670 Reviewed-by: Zach Reizner &lt;zachr@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="353670" owner_email="dgreid@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/70/353670/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I964f5751e3d25cdb824661631444b0b13db37dc0" commit="f2086aa23c65d1ffce8263f3cf7ec6007a3e07f2" commit_message="Add UprevvedChrome flag in metadata.json for Chrome PFQ.  Display whether a PFQ run actually uprevved Chrome in metadata results. If Chrome uprev happens, 'UprevvedChrome: True' will be added to master chrome builder metadata; otherwise, no 'UprevvedChrome' flag will be added to any metadata.  BUG=chromium:619205 TEST=unit_test;cbuildbot  Change-Id: I964f5751e3d25cdb824661631444b0b13db37dc0 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/353166 Commit-Ready: Ningning Xia &lt;nxia@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Ningning Xia &lt;nxia@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Aviv Keshet &lt;akeshet@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="353166" owner_email="nxia@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/chromite" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/chromite" ref="refs/changes/66/353166/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I2b6431ea9fdc0597233c5d1d8c640e2278d8f352" commit="b5d5d0c78a84dbfacf082e86cc9babd9bb850e24" commit_message="ec-utils: fix building usb_updater when BOARD is defined  User environment definition for BOARD is inherited when the ebuild runs, which in turn prevents the usb_updater makefile from using the correct board setting.  The fix is setting BOARD=cr50 explicitly in the ebuild, because usb_updater is not supposed to be built for other ec boards yet.  BUG=none TEST=running &quot;BOARD=kevin emerge-kevin ec-utils&quot; does not fail any       more  Change-Id: I2b6431ea9fdc0597233c5d1d8c640e2278d8f352 Signed-off-by: Vadim Bendebury &lt;vbendeb@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/354773 Reviewed-by: Bill Richardson &lt;wfrichar@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="354773" owner_email="vbendeb@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" ref="refs/changes/73/354773/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ib6f278ad889424f4df85e4a328da1f45c8d00730" commit="20dc6a1ee56df7bd8cdb634ffb4131d760efb4a0" commit_message="Cr50: NvMem: Added mutex lock protection for cache memory  Added mutex lock for nvmem write/move operations. In the current implementation, there is no single entry point for the platform specific NvMem calls. The mutex lock is coupled with a task number so that the same task can attempt to grab the lock without stalling itself.  In addition to the mutex lock, changed where the cache.base_ptr variable is updated. Previously, this was done prior to the partition being copied from flash to the shared memory area. Now, the variable is only updated after the copy so that read operations will always read from the correctly from either flash or from cache memory if a write operation has been started.  BRANCH=none BUG=chrome-os-partner:52520 TEST=Manual make runtests TEST_LIST_HOST=nvmem and verify that all tests pass. Tested with tcg_test utility to test reads/writes using the command &quot;build/test-tpm2/install/bin/compliance --ntpm localhost:9883 --select CPCTPM_TC2_3_33_07_01&quot;.  Change-Id: Ib6f278ad889424f4df85e4a328da1f45c8d00730 Signed-off-by: Scott &lt;scollyer@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/353026 Commit-Ready: Scott Collyer &lt;scollyer@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Scott Collyer &lt;scollyer@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Bill Richardson &lt;wfrichar@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="353026" owner_email="scollyer@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/26/353026/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ic7a5dba5a6f447639dcd1a58b0e640d31f03d5a4" commit="1fa06fddd585c4bd1fb570f2fbd91224c128331b" commit_message="Revert &quot;chromeos-ec: do not build cr50 when making buildall&quot;  CL:354540 skips building cr50 when dependent private folders are not present, so this ebuild modification should no longer be necessary to pass `make buildall` on public builders.  BUG=chromium:621993 BRANCH=None TEST=`emerge-glados chromeos-ec` from public checkout, verify no failure.  Change-Id: Ic7a5dba5a6f447639dcd1a58b0e640d31f03d5a4 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/354771 Commit-Ready: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Vadim Bendebury &lt;vbendeb@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="354771" owner_email="shawnn@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" ref="refs/changes/71/354771/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-2016.05" change_id="Iba573fba9f9b88b87867c6963e48215e254319ed" commit="590ec44b1a590a7dae9c91f05f7ed2288a6be233" commit_message="gru: include ram_code in coreboot table  This is needed to ensure that the ram-code node is included in the device tree by depthcharge.  BRANCH=none BUG=chrome-os-partner:54566 TEST=built updated firmware, booted on kevin into Linux shell, checked      the device tree contents:    localhost ~ # od -tx1 /proc/device-tree/firmware/coreboot/ram-code   0000000 00 00 00 01   0000004   localhost #  Change-Id: Iba573fba9f9b88b87867c6963e48215e254319ed Signed-off-by: Vadim Bendebury &lt;vbendeb@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/354705 Reviewed-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="354705" owner_email="vbendeb@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot" ref="refs/changes/05/354705/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><lkgm version="8487.0.0-rc1"/></manifest>